        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Does Gator Maxx G2 leave a haze?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Alliance markets the G2 formulas as haze-free and dust-free, and says they are not expected to leave a residue. Its FAQ still names damp pavers, high humidity and surface dust as the causes of the occasional haze, and suggests an efflorescence cleaner or a hot-water pressure washer if it happens. Install on dry pavers with the faces blown clean."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Can you use Gator sand over a concrete slab?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Only some of it. Alliance's FAQ says Gator Maxx and Maxx G2 are the only Gator polymeric sands approved for use over concrete, with Maxx G2 rated for overlay joints up to 2 inches and rain-safe after 24 hours there. The overlay also needs proper drainage holes and base details, which Alliance sets out on its product page."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Is Gator better than DOMINATOR?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "On paper they are close. Gator Maxx G2 and DOMINATOR XL are both described as working in joints from 1/8 to 4 inches with a single watering. Gator publishes a clearer rain-safe time and an overlay rating; DOMINATOR offers more colours. On a standard patio, either works well, and the installation matters more than the brand."
          }
        }
      ],
    },
    {
      "@type": "Article",
      headline: title,
      description: description,
      author: authorSchema(Astro.url.pathname),
      datePublished: dates.published,
