    <!-- Body -->
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 pb-16 lg:pb-24">
      <div class="prose prose-lg prose-blue max-w-none text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
        <p class="text-2xl font-medium text-gray-900 mb-8 italic">
          Alliance Gator Maxx G2 is the most versatile bag in the range, rated by its maker for joints from 1/8 inch up to 4 inches and rain-safe 15 minutes after watering. For an ordinary concrete paver patio, though, it is not the only sensible Gator choice, and for irregular flagstone or permeable paving it is not the right one at all.
        </p>

        <TopPick />

        <div id="lineup" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            1. The Gator Lineup, Decoded
          </h2>

          <p>
            Alliance Gator is the jointing-product brand of Alliance Designer Products, and when people search for Gator sand they usually mean one of several different bags. The names overlap, the older versions are still on some shelves, and the joint widths each one is rated for vary from 1 inch to 6 inches. Before comparing Gator with anything else, it is worth getting the family straight, because buying the wrong member of it is the most common way a Gator job goes wrong.
          </p>

          <p>
            This review is built from Alliance's own product pages, technical data and FAQ, cross-checked against dealer listings. We have not run our own installation of these products for this review, so where something is a maker's claim rather than an independently verified fact, we say so. The current jointing products on Alliance's site, and what each is rated for, are below.
          </p>

          <div class="overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl my-8">
            <div class="overflow-x-auto">
              <table class="w-full text-left">
                <thead class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-200">
                  <tr>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Product</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Rated joint width</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Rain-safe after watering</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Best for</th>
                  </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Gator Maxx Sand G2</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">1/8 in to 4 in (2 in on overlays)</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">15 minutes (24 hours on overlays)</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Wide joints, slab overlays, clay pavers</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Gator Supersand Bond G2</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">1/8 in to 2 in</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">15 minutes</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Standard concrete paver patios and walks</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Gator Maxx Sand</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">1/8 in to 2 in</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">1 hour (24 hours on overlays)</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Older-generation Maxx; sold in the USA</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Gator Supersand Bond</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">1/8 in to 1 in</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">1 hour</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Older-generation, residential, narrower joints</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Gator Dust Bond</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">1/2 in to 6 in</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">24 hours</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Irregular flagstone, pedestrian only</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Gator Nitro-X Fine Joint Sand</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">1/8 in to 2 in</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Installed wet; rain delays set</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Resin-bound joints you can install in damp weather</td>
                  </tr>
                </tbody>
              </table>
            </div>
          </div>

          <p>
            Two further products round out the family for permeable work: Gator Nitro Joint Sand, which Alliance describes as permeable, and Gator Aqua Rock, a permeable granite stone for the joints and bedding of permeable paver systems. Alliance also sells Eurostone Sand for natural stone pavers and slabs with joints up to 1.5 inches. For most homeowners, the decision comes down to Maxx G2, Supersand Bond G2 or Gator Dust Bond, and the rest of this review concentrates on those.
          </p>

          <p>
            The G2 label is the important one. It marks the newer formulas, which Alliance markets as dust-free and haze-free, requiring one watering rather than three and no leaf blower, and rain-safe in 15 minutes instead of an hour. If a store is selling Gator Maxx or Gator Supersand without G2 on the bag, you are looking at the earlier generation, which works differently at the watering stage.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="maxx-g2" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            2. Gator Maxx G2: What the Spec Sheet Promises
          </h2>

          <p>
            Gator Maxx Sand G2 is the flagship, and the bag most people mean when they search for Alliance Gator polymeric sand. Alliance describes it as calibrated sand mixed with what it calls intelligent polymers, and lists it for residential and commercial use on concrete pavers, natural stone, wet-cast products and clay pavers, across pool decks, patios, footpaths, driveways and parking areas.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Joint width and depth</h3>

          <p>
            The headline number is range. Alliance rates Maxx G2 for joints from a minimum of 1/8 inch up to 4 inches on a traditional compacted base, and up to 2 inches on an overlay, meaning pavers laid over a concrete slab. It specifies a minimum joint depth of 1 1/2 inches, and says the cured sand must sit at least 1/8 inch below the top of the paver, or below the chamfer on chamfered pavers. That last detail is easy to ignore and important: sand filled flush gets tracked out and erodes.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Colours and coverage</h3>

          <p>
            Alliance's product page lists four colours: Beige, Slate Grey, Ivory and Black Diamond. The 50 lb bag is quoted at 65 to 85 square feet on narrow joints of 1/8 to 3/8 inch, and 22 to 42 square feet on wide joints of 3/4 to 1 1/4 inch, both based on pavers about 2 3/8 inches thick. Black Diamond is quoted slightly lower, at 58 to 76 and 20 to 38 square feet. Those are the maker's figures; real coverage depends on how uniform your joints actually are, so measure several and plan for the widest.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Watering, rain and temperature</h3>

          <p>
            Maxx G2 is activated with a single watering: Alliance says to start at the lowest point and shower at least 30 seconds per 30 square feet, or until the sand repels water and water starts to pool on the joints. It must be installed above 32 degrees Fahrenheit in dry conditions, and Alliance rates it rain-safe 15 minutes after activation on a traditional base, or 24 hours on an overlay. That short rain window is the single most practical advantage of the G2 formula; with older polymeric sands, an unexpected shower an hour after watering can undo the job.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Warranty</h3>

          <p>
            Alliance offers a 15-year limited warranty for residential use and one year for commercial, mixed-use or institutional jobs. Read the terms before you rely on them. The remedy is a refund of the purchase price with proof of purchase, it applies only when the product was installed to the specified method, and it excludes removal and reinstallation costs. In other words, it covers the bags, not the weekend you spent installing them, and not the labour to redo a failed patio.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="other-bags" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            3. Supersand, Gator Dust and Nitro-X: When Maxx G2 Is Not the Bag
          </h2>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Gator Supersand Bond G2</h3>

          <p>
            For a typical concrete paver patio or walkway, Supersand Bond G2 does the same job across the joint widths most people actually have. Alliance rates it for joints from 1/8 inch to 2 inches, with the same 1 1/2 inch minimum depth, the same one-watering activation, the same 15-minute rain-safe rating, the same coverage figures per 50 lb bag, and the same 15-year residential warranty. It comes in two colours, Beige and Slate Grey. What it does not offer is Maxx G2's wider top end or its overlay rating; Alliance's FAQ states that only Gator Maxx and Maxx G2 are approved over concrete.
          </p>

          <p>
            If your joints are all under 2 inches and your pavers sit on a compacted gravel base, Supersand Bond G2 is the sensible Gator to compare on price. If you have pavers over a slab, irregular wide joints, or want Ivory or Black Diamond, Maxx G2 is the one.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">The older Maxx and Supersand Bond</h3>

          <p>
            The non-G2 versions are still listed. Gator Maxx Sand is rated to 2 inches on both traditional and overlay bases and is marked as sold in the USA only; Gator Supersand Bond is rated to 1 inch. Both use a multi-step watering sequence (three waterings, in the case of Maxx), both are rain-safe after one hour rather than 15 minutes, and Alliance's own pages say that if the watering steps are skipped or done poorly, a white haze may appear on the paver surface. They are not bad products, but if you are buying new, the G2 formulas are the ones Alliance itself positions as the improvement.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Gator Dust Bond for flagstone</h3>

          <p>
            Irregular flagstone with joints that wander from half an inch to several inches is a different job, and Gator Dust Bond is Alliance's answer. It is a polymeric stone dust rated for joints from 1/2 inch to 6 inches, for pedestrian use only, in Sahara Beige and a grey. The installation conditions are stricter: Alliance says to install it only above 59 degrees Fahrenheit, with no rain forecast for the 24 hours before installation, and rates it rain-safe 24 hours after watering. Alliance's FAQ also says Gator Dust should never be used on clay pavers.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Gator Nitro-X Fine Joint Sand</h3>

          <p>
            Nitro-X is not a conventional polymeric sand. Alliance calls it an air-cure, low-permeability jointing material made from fine sand and a proprietary resin, sold in a 27.5 lb pail rather than a bag. It is installed onto a pre-wetted surface, can be applied rain or shine above 37 degrees Fahrenheit, has about 45 minutes of working time, takes foot traffic after roughly 18 hours in warm, dry conditions and reaches full strength in 28 days. It carries a 5-year residential warranty rather than 15. It suits someone whose real problem is finding a dry enough day, and it is rated for light-use residential driveways.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="haze-and-cure" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. Haze, Cure and Soft Joints: How Gator Behaves
          </h2>

          <p>
            Haze is the failure everyone fears with polymeric sand, so Alliance's claims here deserve a careful reading. For the G2 products, the marketing is unambiguous: Alliance says the new generation of intelligent polymers neutralises haze and dust, and calls the application dust-free and haze-free. Its FAQ is more measured. It lists high humidity, damp pavers and dust left on the surface as the causes of the rare cases of hazing, says G2 products are not expected to leave a residue, and recommends making sure the pavers are completely dry and blowing dust off from several directions. If haze does appear, Alliance points to an efflorescence cleaner, a hot-water pressure washer or simply weathering. Our guide to <a href="/how-to-remove-polymeric-sand-haze-from-pavers/">removing polymeric sand haze</a> covers those methods.
          </p>

          <p>
            The sensible reading is that G2 widens the margin for error rather than eliminating it. A damp clay paver, a humid morning or a dusty surface can still cause trouble, which is why Alliance tells clay-paver installers to be sure the clay is 100 per cent dry and to test a small area first.
          </p>

          <p>
            The second thing worth understanding before you install is that cured Gator sand is not supposed to be rock hard all the time. Alliance's FAQ says plainly that polymeric sand is not a mortar and is designed to transition from hard and dry to soft when wet, and that a soft area simply means that area is still damp. A thin surface crust is described as normal, acting like a hinge that helps the joint cope with freeze-thaw. People who press a thumb into a joint the morning after rain and conclude the product has failed are often just seeing it work as designed. When joints stay soft for days in dry weather, the usual cause is under-watering or trapped water, which our guide to <a href="/why-is-my-polymeric-sand-not-hardening/">polymeric sand that is not hardening</a> works through.
          </p>

          <p>
            Cracking gets a similarly frank answer. Alliance says that if a polymeric sand is too hard it can also be too brittle, cracking when the ground moves, and that there is no accepted industry test method for the strength and durability of polymeric sand. That is worth keeping in mind whenever any brand, Alliance included, calls its sand the strongest: there is no standard test behind the word. Cracks in a Gator joint almost always trace back to rocking pavers or a moving base rather than to the sand itself.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-3">READ THE FINE PRINT</p>
            <p class="text-xl font-bold text-white mb-3">Haze-free is a claim. Dry pavers are a requirement.</p>
            <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed m-0">Alliance markets its G2 sands as haze-free and dust-free, but its own FAQ still lists damp pavers, humidity and surface dust as the causes of haze. Treat the claim as extra margin, and install on a dry day with the faces blown clean anyway.</p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div id="owners" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. What Owners Run Into
          </h2>

          <p>
            Honest owner feedback on Gator is harder to pin down than the marketing. Gator is sold mainly through hardscape dealers and landscape suppliers, with some online listings, so it does not have the thousands of big-box reviews that shelf brands accumulate, and the retail review counts on the listings we checked were too small to draw a pattern from. What we can say with confidence is what the problems look like, because Alliance's own FAQ is essentially a catalogue of them, and they match the failure reports that recur across polymeric sand in general.
          </p>

          <p>
            The most common complaints are joints that feel soft, joints that never seem to harden, and a white film on the pavers. Alliance's answers trace almost all of them to installation: watering too little, installing on damp pavers, leaving dust on the surface, or rain arriving before the sand set. For under-watered joints, the FAQ suggests running a sprinkler for about an hour, poking small holes through any crust so the water can get in, and removing and reinstalling if the sand still has not hardened after several days of good weather.
          </p>

          <p>
            The next group of problems is about depth. Alliance says that skipping compaction can leave 30 to 50 per cent of the sand missing from the joint, which shows up later as sunken, crumbling joints. It also warns against topping, meaning filling the bottom of a joint with regular sand and finishing with polymeric, which it says can cause failure and voids the warranty. Both are covered alongside the rest of the common errors in our guide to <a href="/polymeric-sand-installation-mistakes/">polymeric sand installation mistakes</a>.
          </p>

          <p>
            Finally, there are the problems no sand can fix: moss, mould and mildew in shady, damp spots, which Alliance says its product will neither cause nor prevent, and cracking from pavers that rock or a base that has settled. If your patio has either, deal with the drainage or the base before blaming, or buying, the sand. On a sound base, installed on a dry day and watered properly, Alliance says its polymeric sands should last about 15 years; that is the maker's estimate, and the warranty terms above show how it backs it.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="versus" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. Gator vs DOMINATOR vs Techniseal
          </h2>

          <p>
            Gator's two most common rivals in premium polymeric sand are DOMINATOR and Techniseal, and all three are capable products. The useful differences are in joint range, the watering method, what each maker documents, and where you can actually buy the bag.
          </p>

          <p>
            DOMINATOR, covered in detail in our <a href="/dominator-polymeric-sand-review/">DOMINATOR polymeric sand review</a>, now appears at Lowe's as DOMINATOR XL in 40 lb bags, which the listings describe as working in joints from 1/8 to 4 inches with a single watering and a zero-dust formula, in a wider spread of colours than Gator's four. On paper, then, its joint range matches Maxx G2. We could not find a published rain-safe time for it in the listings we checked, which makes Gator's 15-minute figure the better documented of the two.
          </p>

          <p>
            Techniseal's HP NextGel takes a slightly different approach. Retailer listings quoting Techniseal describe its NextGel additive as making the sand practically dust-free and as stopping additive particles from penetrating the pores of the pavers, which Techniseal identifies as the main cause of haze, and several retailers list a 20-year warranty. Pine Hall Brick, a clay paver maker, names HP NextGel as one of two polymeric sands it has found less prone to hazing on clay brick. For tight joints on clay, that third-party endorsement is worth weighing against Gator.
          </p>

          <div class="overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl my-8">
            <div class="overflow-x-auto">
