          <p>
            The honest conclusion is that the brand matters less than matching the product to the joint and the base. On a standard concrete paver patio, any of the three, installed properly on a dry day, should give you firm, clean joints. Gator's advantages are its clearly documented spec sheets, the short rain window on G2, and an overlay rating for pavers on a slab. Its drawbacks are a narrow colour range and dealer-led distribution. For the full field ranked, see our guide to the <a href="/best-polymeric-sand/">best polymeric sand</a>.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="verdict" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            7. Who Should Buy Which Gator Bag, and Who Should Not
          </h2>

          <p>
            Buy Gator Maxx G2 if your joints run wider than 2 inches, if your pavers are laid over a concrete slab, if you need Ivory or Black Diamond, or if the weather where you live makes a 15-minute rain window genuinely valuable. It is the most flexible bag in the range and the one Alliance itself calls its most resilient.
          </p>

          <p>
            Buy Gator Supersand Bond G2 if you have an ordinary concrete paver patio, walk or driveway on a compacted base with joints under 2 inches and you are happy with Beige or Slate Grey. On paper it gives you the same watering, rain-safe time, coverage and residential warranty as Maxx G2 for the joints most patios actually have.
          </p>

          <p>
            Buy Gator Dust Bond for irregular natural flagstone with wide, uneven joints on a path or patio that only sees foot traffic, and only when you can install above 59 degrees Fahrenheit with a dry day either side. Consider Nitro-X if your problem is that the forecast never gives you a dry surface to work on.
          </p>

          <p>
            Skip Gator, and polymeric sand in general, in a few situations. On a permeable paver system, a standard polymeric sand seals joints that are meant to drain; use the open-graded material the system specifies, such as Alliance's own Aqua Rock or Nitro. On clay brick laid with joints narrower than 1/8 inch, every Gator polymeric bag is below its rated minimum, and a fine dry joint sand is often the better answer, as covered in our guide to <a href="/silica-sand-for-pavers/">silica sand for pavers</a>. And on a patio whose pavers rock or whose base has sunk, fix the base first; no jointing product survives a moving surface.
          </p>

          <p>
            Whatever you buy, the method matters more than the bag. Our step-by-step guide to <a href="/how-to-use-polymeric-sand/">how to use polymeric sand</a> covers the sweeping, compaction and watering sequence, and Alliance's own instructions on the bag override anything general when the two differ.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div id="mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            8. Common Mistakes to Avoid
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Buying by Brand Instead of Joint Width</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Gator spans products rated from 1 inch to 6 inches, and the wrong one fails no matter how well it is installed. Measure several joints, including the widest, then pick the bag rated for them: Supersand Bond G2 to 2 inches, Maxx G2 to 4 inches, Gator Dust Bond for wide flagstone joints.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Installing on Damp Pavers</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Even the G2 formulas Alliance markets as haze-free list damp pavers and humidity as the causes of haze. Clay pavers are the worst offenders, because they can hold moisture while looking dry. Wait for a dry surface, sweep and blow the faces clean, and test a small area first.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Skipping Compaction</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Alliance says uncompacted joints can be missing 30 to 50 per cent of the sand they need, which shows up later as sunken, crumbling joints. Run a plate compactor with a protective pad, top up, and repeat until the joints stay full and the sand sits about 1/8 inch below the paver surface.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Topping Old Sand With Gator</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Filling the bottom of a joint with regular sand and finishing it with polymeric sand leaves a thin crust over loose fill. Alliance calls this topping, says it can cause failure, and says it voids the warranty. Remove the old material to at least 1 1/2 inches before installing new sand.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Expecting the Warranty to Cover Labour</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Alliance's 15-year residential warranty refunds the purchase price of the product, with proof of purchase, when it was installed to the specified method. It excludes removal and reinstallation costs. Keep your receipt and follow the bag exactly, but do not treat the warranty as insurance on the job itself.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            9. Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is Alliance Gator polymeric sand any good?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Gator is one of the better-documented premium polymeric sands, with clear joint-width ratings, a 15-minute rain-safe window on its G2 formulas and an overlay rating on Maxx G2. Its weaknesses are a narrow colour range and dealer-led availability. Like every polymeric sand, it performs only as well as the installation: dry pavers, full compaction and correct watering.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What is the difference between Gator Maxx G2 and Supersand Bond G2?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Both use the G2 one-watering formula, are rain-safe 15 minutes after activation and share the same coverage figures and residential warranty. Maxx G2 is rated for joints up to 4 inches, is approved over concrete slabs and comes in four colours. Supersand Bond G2 tops out at 2 inches, is not for slab overlays and comes in Beige and Slate Grey.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How long does Gator Maxx G2 take to cure before rain?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Alliance rates Gator Maxx G2 rain-safe 15 minutes after water activation on a traditional compacted base, and 24 hours on an overlay over concrete. It must be installed above 32 degrees Fahrenheit in dry conditions. Alliance's FAQ says you can walk or drive on it after 24 hours if it is dry.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How much does a 50 lb bag of Gator Maxx G2 cover?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Alliance quotes 65 to 85 square feet on narrow joints of 1/8 to 3/8 inch and 22 to 42 square feet on wide joints of 3/4 to 1 1/4 inch, for pavers about 2 3/8 inches thick. Black Diamond is quoted slightly lower. Real coverage depends on joint uniformity, so buy an extra bag.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Does Gator Maxx G2 leave a haze?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Alliance markets the G2 formulas as haze-free and dust-free, and says they are not expected to leave a residue. Its FAQ still names damp pavers, high humidity and surface dust as the causes of the occasional haze, and suggests an efflorescence cleaner or a hot-water pressure washer if it happens. Install on dry pavers with the faces blown clean.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can you use Gator sand over a concrete slab?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Only some of it. Alliance's FAQ says Gator Maxx and Maxx G2 are the only Gator polymeric sands approved for use over concrete, with Maxx G2 rated for overlay joints up to 2 inches and rain-safe after 24 hours there. The overlay also needs proper drainage holes and base details, which Alliance sets out on its product page.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is Gator better than DOMINATOR?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                On paper they are close. Gator Maxx G2 and DOMINATOR XL are both described as working in joints from 1/8 to 4 inches with a single watering. Gator publishes a clearer rain-safe time and an overlay rating; DOMINATOR offers more colours. On a standard patio, either works well, and the installation matters more than the brand.
              </p>
            </div>

          </div>
        </div>

